Fallible casts use the same plain `cast` expression as infallible casts. Their
actual failure follows ordinary implicit or typed error delivery.

### Ordinary failure delivery

An invalid value for a supported conversion produces recoverable failure. A
private function can carry implicit failure through its existing local
failure route. A compatible builtin `Error!` slot can return it. A custom
`E!` slot does not automatically convert builtin failure.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    parse_count |text String| -> I32, Error!:
        return cast text
    ;
]

A source-authored fallible evidence method exposes its declared `Error!`
result. The caller still writes plain `cast`. Postfix `!` remains for ordinary
fallible calls and does not attach to a cast.

### Local recovery

Use `catch` to recover a fallible conversion:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    count I32 = cast text catch:
        then 0
    ;

    other_count I32 = cast text catch |err|:
        io.line(err.message)
        then 0
    ;
]

The handler produces the success values required by the receiving site or
leaves through `return` or `return!`. It protects both operand evaluation and
conversion:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    narrow |left U32, right U32| -> U8:
        value U8 = cast left + right catch then 0
        return value
    ;
]

This catches `U32` addition overflow and failure to convert its result to
`U8`. The addition still computes in its own selected domain.

Implicit failure and compatible builtin `Error!` outcomes can share a handler.
A bound error has type `Error`. Existing typed `Error` values keep their
message, code and identity, including code `0`. A custom typed error cannot
share a handler with implicit builtin failure or another distinct error type.
Compatible typed calls inside the protected expression need no per-call
postfix marker.

### Expression-wide scope

`catch` protects the complete selected expression, including arithmetic,
receivers, arguments, nested calls and cast operands. Parentheses do not shrink
an outer handler:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    amount = load_amount(left * right + offset) catch then 0
]

The expression evaluates once in ordinary source order. The first failure
skips later protected work and enters the handler. Earlier mutations and IO
remain visible. A catch is not a transaction, and failure in its handler goes
outwards rather than re-entering it.

A catch on a compound assignment's right-hand expression protects only that
expression. The later compound arithmetic and any checked conversion back
remain separate failure points. The store commits only after every check
succeeds.

### Cast evidence and static failures

Evidence follows the complete source-target pair, not a value the compiler
happens to know. A conversion that can fail for that type pair follows ordinary
failure delivery even when this particular value proves safe. A compiler proof
may remove a runtime check but cannot change the evidence class.

An authored cast can qualify a safety catch even when the conversion is
infallible. That eligibility does not fabricate an error source. A `catch`
cannot legalise an unsupported pair, missing evidence or statically invalid
numeric operation. Known failures remain diagnostics, even with a catch or a
builtin `Error!` slot.

An optional `T?` receiver casts or recovers inner `T`, then wraps the result.
The handler produces `T`, so `then none` remains invalid. Optional source
values do not unwrap automatically.

See @./#cast-evidence (Cast evidence), @./#cast-targets (Cast targets) and
@../errors/#catch-and-recovery (Catch and recovery). The Progress matrix owns
current compiler and backend support: @../progress (Progress).
